      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to the Free Software Movement</strong><br>\nRichard Stallman's contributions to the free software movement go beyond the GNU Project. He has written extensively on the subject, advocating for software freedoms and the ethical implications of software ownership. Stallman's activism has influenced the development of free software and the debates around digital rights.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Creation of Emacs</strong><br>\nOne of Stallman's significant contributions is the creation of Emacs, an extensible, customizable text editor. First released in 1976, Emacs has become an essential tool for many programmers. It is known for its powerful editing features and has been ported to many computing systems.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Establishment of the Free Software Foundation</strong><br>\nIn 1985, Stallman established the Free Software Foundation (FSF), an organization dedicated to promoting computer users' rights to use, study, distribute, and modify computer software. The FSF advocates for the development and use of free (as in freedom) software—not to be confused with \"freeware,\" which is simply software available at no cost.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
